>     * The Monk class

Cute, but really could've stood an overhaul.

>     * Sokoban

Should have never been added to Nethack.

>     * Conduct (voluntary challenges)

Many of which are just an apology for not fixing extant design flaws.

>     * Riding

Poorly implemented from a number of perspectives:

-- Requires a super-rare item, the saddle; meaning you either start with
it (as a Knight), wish for it, or end up not riding in most games
-- The one role who starts with a saddle can't effectively use it right
away (and there is no warning of this other than "You slip while
mounting... You die")
-- Jousting requires intimate knowledge of the internals of the game to
be effective (you need to know how to make it safe to use so you don't break
your lance, another rare item)
-- Once you _know_ those internals, jousting is broken due to poor speed
implementation

>     * Two-weapon combat

Instantly obsoleted shields and increased the player's damage output
sufficiently such that many monsters were simply no longer threatening;
is _always_ the correct answer unless your role can't do it or your only
source of reflection is the shield.

Yep, you sure can see where these ideas came from Slash'EM, all right.

If they had been properly integrated, some of them are still good ideas
(riding, twoweapon), but porting 'em over straight from Slash'EM was,
well, a pretty big mistake.  Slash'EM is what it is, and if you like it,
fine, but it is _not_ balanced well at all.

